menu "Person Detection Example"

    config PERSON_DETECT_VIDEO_WIDTH
        int "Capture width"
        default 1280
        help
            One profile serves the whole example - the encoder and WebRTC both
            describe this. The preview and the detector scale from it themselves
            and are unaffected.

            This is a REQUEST, and on the P4 CSI path the sensor has the last word: the
            video device is bound to the sensor's compiled default format and rejects
            VIDIOC_S_FMT for any other size, so changing this alone silently keeps the
            old resolution (esp_video_if falls back and logs a warning). Change the
            matching CONFIG_CAMERA_<sensor>_MIPI_DEFAULT_FMT_* choice with it.

    config PERSON_DETECT_VIDEO_HEIGHT
        int "Capture height"
        default 720

    config PERSON_DETECT_VIDEO_FPS
        int "Capture frame rate"
        range 1 60
        default 30

    config PERSON_DETECT_VIDEO_BITRATE
        int "Requested bitrate (kbps)"
        range 100 8000
        default 1000
        help
            Carried in video_capture_config_t.

            It does NOT set the encoder's bitrate ceiling, despite how it reads: on the
            P4 CSI path esp32p4_frame_grabber.c derives that from the resolution instead
            (LINK_BASE_BITRATE_BPS * height / 720, so 1 Mbps at 720p and 1.5 Mbps at
            1080p) and overwrites whatever arrived here. That ceiling is the top of the
            rate-control ladder; the floor is VIDEO_RATE_CTRL_MIN_MBPS_PER_PX. Keep this
            value near the derived one so the two do not tell different stories.

    config PERSON_DETECT_FPS
        int "Inference rate (frames per second)"
        range 1 30
        default 5
        help
            How many camera frames per second are handed to the model. The rest
            are dropped by the bus before they are ever queued, which is much
            cheaper than receiving 30 and discarding 25.

            pedestrian_detect takes roughly 60 ms per frame on the P4, so the
            model itself tops out near 16 fps. 5 is a sensible default: it keeps
            detection responsive while leaving the CPU to the encoder and the
            preview.

            This costs nothing in camera buffers - the inference sink is a
            converted sink, so the PPA hands it a small private frame and the
            camera buffer goes straight back.

    config PERSON_DETECT_PREVIEW_FPS
        int "LCD preview rate (frames per second)"
        range 1 30
        default 5
        help
            How often the LCD preview is refreshed. Independent of the inference
            rate, which is the point: the preview stays smooth while detection
            runs slowly behind it.

            Because the two are asynchronous, the boxes on screen belong to an
            earlier frame than the picture under them - by roughly one inference
            period. That is expected.

    config PERSON_DETECT_SCORE_THRESHOLD
        int "Detection score threshold (percent)"
        range 1 99
        default 70
        help
            Minimum confidence for a detection to be reported, as a percentage.
            The model's own default is 70.

            Lower catches more distant or partly occluded people at the cost of
            false positives; higher is stricter.

    config PERSON_DETECT_ENABLE_AT_BOOT
        bool "Start inference at boot"
        default y
        help
            When set, the inference sink starts enabled. When clear it is
            registered but idle until "detect on" is typed at the console, which
            is useful for measuring what inference actually costs the rest of the
            pipeline.

            The preview is unaffected either way.

endmenu
